WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.792
Securing AI agents begins with understanding the complete system.

2
00:00:03.792 --> 00:00:12.871
Agents combine model reasoning with instructions, tools, APIs, data, memory, identities, workflows, and automated actions.

3
00:00:12.871 --> 00:00:15.994
Their security cannot depend on model behavior alone.

4
00:00:15.994 --> 00:00:23.568
Teams need clear trust boundaries and controls at the points where language becomes access, data movement, or system change.

5
00:00:23.568 --> 00:00:26.611
The core design principles reinforce one another.

6
00:00:26.611 --> 00:00:43.443
Treat external content as untrusted, enforce instruction authority outside the model, scope tools through least privilege, authorize data at retrieval time, control memory, protect credentials, and separate low-risk assistance from high-impact execution.

7
00:00:43.443 --> 00:00:50.976
Use meaningful human approval gates when an action is sensitive, difficult to reverse, or outside a well-bounded task.

8
00:00:50.976 --> 00:00:53.511
Operational controls complete the design.

9
00:00:53.511 --> 00:01:02.550
Log tool calls, approvals, data access, policy decisions, outcomes, and errors without exposing unnecessary sensitive information.

10
00:01:02.550 --> 00:01:13.166
Monitor for unusual behavior, prepare to pause agents and revoke access, test expected and denied actions, and review the system whenever capabilities change.

11
00:01:13.166 --> 00:01:19.213
Governance provides ownership, inventory, risk decisions, and evidence of continuing oversight.

12
00:01:19.213 --> 00:01:26.427
Agent autonomy should always be matched with permissions, monitoring, testing, and human review appropriate to the impact.

13
00:01:26.427 --> 00:01:35.167
Secure systems assume that models can misunderstand, external content can mislead, dependencies can fail, and workflows can change.

14
00:01:35.167 --> 00:01:43.120
Layered controls keep those imperfections from becoming unauthorized or harmful outcomes while preserving the productive value of agents.
